    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d7">

                <pb facs="0016" n="4" sameAs="frus:frus1943v03/pg4"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">033.4111 Eden, Anthony/1<hi
                        rendition="#superscript">3/5</hi>: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Matthews</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-02-18T15:00:00+01:00">February 18,
                            1943—3 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received February 18—11:03 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1246. My telegram number 1171, February 15, 9 p.m. I learn that in addition to
                    those mentioned Strang<anchor n="14" xml:id="d7-f1"/> will likewise accompany
                    Mr. Eden. In mentioning this Sir Orme Sargent<anchor n="15" xml:id="d7-f2"/>
                    expressed some little anxiety less [<hi rendition="#italic">lest</hi>] the size
                    and composition of Mr. Eden’s party might lead to public expectations on both
                    sides of the Atlantic that important negotiations are on foot and subsequent
                    disappointment and harmful speculation when no great tangible results of the
                    talks in Washington can be announced.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Matthews</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d7-f1" n="14" xml:id="d7-f1-content">
                <p>William Strang, British Assistant Under Secretary of State for Foreign
